    "body": "<!-- claude-projects-attribution: {\"github_login\":\"siainbuletin\"} -->\n_Requested by **Sia** · [project thread](https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2T8E7LhF3ZQzmWj4pm3aP7Z)_\n\n## Summary\nThe author and litnote lint commands now live in the `sp-org-lint` doc, `docs/tangles/funct/sp-org-lint.org`, one section each (built on main after #224 merged):\n\n- `sp-author-notes.el` and `sp-author-notes-bibtex.el` (their own docs are deleted),\n- `sp-litnotes-refs.el` and `sp-litnotes-titles.el` (taken out of `sp-academic.org`).\n\nThe table of contents at the top of `sp-academic.org` still lists all four, marked \"Author lint\" / \"Litnote lint\", with links to the new sections and a sentence naming the commands. `sp-org-lint.org` has a matching table in its intro. README, CHANGELOG and the `.el` docstrings point to the new place.\n\n## Decision for you\nI moved the **docs**, not the code into one `.el`. Each section still tangles to its own file, so the `requires` (`sp-litnotes`, `init.el`) are untouched, and the footnote worker (`emacs -Q --batch -l sp-org-lint.el`) keeps loading a small file instead of ~3000 more lines. If you want a single `sp-org-lint.el`, it is a mechanical follow-up, but the worker would then load everything.\n\n## Test plan\n- [x] `tools/check-tangle.sh` in Emacs 29.3: the only differences are the two docstring paths committed here.\n- [x] ERT: author-notes 18, author-notes-bibtex 4, litnotes-refs 6, litnotes-titles 8 pass.\n- [ ] CI tangle check; open `sp-org-lint.org` in Emacs and check the folding and links.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01WWLNiBdNucJCEhP3kY1oNm)_",
